    "question": "Sophia ate 1/6 of her pie and she put the rest on the fridge. If the pie left in the fridge weighs 1200 grams, how many grams did Sophia eat?",
    "gold": "240",
    "answer": "If Sophia ate 1/6 of the pie, then 6/6 - 1/6 = 5/6 is left in the fridge.\nLet x be the pie's original weight.\nThe current weight of the pie can be described by 5x/6=1200 grams\nSo, 5x=7200.\nAnd, the original weight is x = <<1440=1440>>1440 grams.\nSo, Sophia ate 1440 grams original - 1200 grams after= <<1440-1200=240>>240 grams of pie.\n#### 240"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine how many grams of pie Sophia ate, we start by understanding the relationship between the fraction of the pie she ate and the weight of the pie left in the fridge.\n\n1. Let the total weight of the pie be \\( W \\) grams.\n2. Sophia ate \\( \\frac{1}{6} \\) of the pie, so the weight of the pie she ate is \\( \\frac{1}{6}W \\).\n3. The weight of the pie left in the fridge is given as 1200 grams. This weight is the remaining part of the pie after Sophia ate her portion. Therefore, the weight of the pie left in the fridge is \\( W - \\frac{1}{6}W \\).\n4. Simplifying the expression for the weight of the pie left in the fridge, we get:\n   \\[\n   W - \\frac{1}{6}W = \\frac{6}{6}W - \\frac{1}{6}W = \\frac{5}{6}W\n   \\]\n   We know that this weight is 1200 grams, so we can set up the equation:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{5}{6}W = 1200\n   \\]\n5. To find \\( W \\), we solve the equation by multiplying both sides by the reciprocal of \\( \\frac{5}{6} \\), which is \\( \\frac{6}{5} \\):\n   \\[\n   W = 1200 \\times \\frac{6}{5} = 1200 \\times 1.2 = 1440\n   \\]\n   So, the total weight of the pie is 1440 grams.\n6. Since Sophia ate \\( \\frac{1}{6} \\) of the pie, the weight of the pie she ate is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{6} \\times 1440 = 240\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of grams of pie that Sophia ate is \\(\\boxed{240}\\).",
